# Copy to `config/e2e.local.env` (GITIGNORED) to customise the e2e run.
# Loaded after config/e2e.env and overrides it, so you only need the keys you
# actually want to change.
#
# Nothing in `config/e2e.local.env` is ever committed — real credentials go
# here, never in config/e2e.env.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The database is NOT configurable, by design.
#
# The suite always runs against the throwaway container from
# docker-compose.e2e.yml. `assertEphemeralDatabase()` refuses to start against
# anything else — including from this file — because a suite that can reach a
# real database eventually will. `.env.development` here points at a hosted
# Neon instance, which is exactly the accident being prevented.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Run with your real data -----------------------------------------------
# Load the real seed INSIDE the ephemeral container. The data is real; the
# database is still thrown away on `npm run e2e:db:down`.
# E2E_SEED_SCRIPT="initial_load/seed.ts"

# --- Run as your real accounts ---------------------------------------------
# Only meaningful together with the real seed above, since these users must
# exist in the container. db.setup.ts never rewrites the password of an
# account it did not create.
# E2E_PASSWORD="..."
# E2E_ADMIN_EMAIL="..."
# E2E_FSR_EMAIL="..."
# E2E_CLIENT_EMAIL="..."
# E2E_GUEST_EMAIL="..."

# --- Ports ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Change both together: the guard checks DATABASE_URL against E2E_DB_PORT.
# E2E_DB_PORT=5433
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://opustrack_e2e:opustrack_e2e@localhost:5433/opustrack_e2e?schema=public"
# PLAYWRIGHT_PORT=3100
# NEXTAUTH_URL="http://localhost:3100"
